$NetBSD$

Experimental GPL XFS port for NetBSD
===================================

This starts a direct port of SGI/Linux XFS, using Linux v6.12 as a pinned
baseline. See README.import for source provenance and COPYING for the license.
The old http://oss.sgi.com/projects/xfs/ URL is historical; the maintained
implementation is in Linux's fs/xfs tree.

Current milestone
-----------------

The upstream superblock decoder, version checks and quota conversion run
through a small NetBSD compatibility header. A read-only probe adds bounded
input handling, v5 CRC32C verification over the complete sector, basic
geometry validation and data-device bounds checks. It handles unaligned
input, checks disk-controlled shifts, and preserves the caller's output on
failure. Filesystem block numbers are decoded as AG/block pairs, including
when the allocation-group size is not a power of two.

The probe recognizes v4 superblocks with v2 directories and unwritten extent
support, and v5 superblocks with the pinned upstream's feature definitions.
It rejects unknown incompatible bits, NEEDSREPAIR, nonzero log-incompatible
bits, incomplete mkfs state, external logs and realtime devices. Unknown
compatible and read-only-compatible bits do not prevent superblock inspection.
Recognizing a feature bit does not mean that its inode or btree format has
been ported.

This is NOT a mountable filesystem or a complete upstream superblock verifier.
It does not check AG headers, inode allocation, directory contents, all log
geometry, or journal cleanliness. Even a read-only mount can require recovery;
a successful probe must never be used as evidence that replay can be skipped.
There is no VFS registration, mount helper, kernel module, or write path yet.
The command-line probe accepts regular image files only and opens them
read-only. No test mounts a filesystem or accesses a raw disk device.

Layout
------

    dist/       Unmodified upstream files, retained for comparison.
    include/    Compatibility definitions and the probe interface.
    netbsd/     Selected upstream routines and NetBSD probe wrapper.
    tests/      Host tests, image inspector and kernel-header compile check.

Build and test from the src root
-------------------------------

    sh sys/external/gpl2/xfs/tests/run.sh
    sh sys/external/gpl2/xfs/tests/run.sh --images
    sh sys/external/gpl2/xfs/tests/check-kernel.sh

The first command needs a C compiler and runs 1,070 checks, including v4 quota
translation, UUID selection, unaligned and short input, sector corruption,
invalid geometry and feature masks, and shortened final allocation groups.
CC and CFLAGS can override the compiler and host-test flags.

The second additionally requires Python 3.11 or newer and xfsprogs (mkfs.xfs
and xfs_db). It creates four disposable 400 MiB sparse images, compares the
probe's fields with xfs_db, checks whole-image hashes before and after probing,
and tests seven damaged/unsupported inputs. The image configurations cover
defaults, 4 KiB sectors, 64 KiB blocks and 2 KiB inodes. Tests were run with
xfsprogs 7.1.1. A future mkfs default may enable a newer, unsupported feature;
review the upstream baseline rather than bypassing the rejection.

The third compiles both port files with this checkout's NetBSD amd64 kernel
headers and no host system headers. It needs an amd64 compiler. This checks
header/layout compatibility only, not linking, module loading or booting.

An undefined-behavior check that does not require sanitizer runtime libraries:

    CFLAGS='-O1 -g -fsanitize=undefined -fsanitize-undefined-trap-on-error' \
        sh sys/external/gpl2/xfs/tests/run.sh

To build a persistent image inspector on NetBSD or Linux:

    xfs_src=sys/external/gpl2/xfs
    cc -std=gnu11 -D_DEFAULT_SOURCE -O2 -I "$xfs_src/include" \
        -isystem "$xfs_src/dist" "$xfs_src/netbsd/xfs_sb.c" \
        "$xfs_src/netbsd/xfs_probe.c" "$xfs_src/tests/xfs_probe.c" \
        -o /tmp/xfs_probe
    /tmp/xfs_probe /path/to/image

Next milestones
---------------

1. Add an explicit block-I/O interface and port upstream AG and buffer
   verifiers. Test reads, I/O errors, UUID/CRC mismatches and all block bounds
   in userland/rump before connecting a live kernel device.
2. Port inode, extent/bmap and directory readers. Test file data, sparse and
   unwritten extents, symlinks, large files and corrupted metadata against
   images populated with Linux XFS. Establish reliable clean-log detection;
   reject filesystems requiring replay until recovery is implemented.
3. Add NetBSD VFS/vnode operations, vnode lifecycle/locking, UBC integration,
   a rump component, a mount helper and an explicitly enabled GPL module.
   Start with read-only mounts and unmount/error-path tests. Unsupported
   formats and write requests must fail rather than use placeholder handlers.
4. Port transaction reservations, logging/recovery and allocation before
   enabling any writes. Require crash/replay tests and xfstests coverage,
   including concurrency and forced-shutdown paths.

Useful references
-----------------

    https://wiki.netbsd.org/projects/project/xfs/
    https://docs.kernel.org/filesystems/xfs/index.html
    https://www.kernel.org/pub/linux/utils/fs/xfs/docs/xfs_filesystem_structure.pdf

NetBSD's existing fs/efs and fs/ntfs code, sys/rump/fs components and
sys/modules layouts provide local integration examples. The XFS transaction,
locking and cache contracts still require explicit adaptation; the scalar
compatibility header is not a substitute for that work.
